---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const codeExecutionCode = `from google.adk.agents.llm_agent import Agent
from google.adk.code_executors.agent_engine_sandbox_code_executor import (
    AgentEngineSandboxCodeExecutor,
)

root_agent = Agent(
    model="gemini-2.5-flash",
    name="diagnostics_code_agent",
    instruction=(
        "Use code to inspect logs and sensor data. "
        "Summarize calculations and cite the files used."
    ),
    code_executor=AgentEngineSandboxCodeExecutor(
        sandbox_resource_name="SANDBOX_RESOURCE_NAME",
    ),
)`;
---

<SiteLayout current="code-execution" title="Code execution">
  <section class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Tool form 03C</p>
      <h1>Code execution makes observations concrete.</h1>
      <p>
        It lets the agent measure instead of guess: run tests, inspect files,
        transform data, calculate results, and verify that a change actually
        works.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="signal-panel signal-panel--two signal-panel--definitions" style="margin-top: 32px; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div>
        <article>
          <span>Use when</span>
          <strong>The answer needs computation.</strong>
          <p>Reach for code execution when the task involves files, logs, tests, charts, data cleaning, repo inspection, simulations, or any result that should be checked rather than asserted.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="pill-avoid">Security</span>
          <strong>Hermetic sandboxing is mandatory.</strong>
          <p>Model-generated code can compromise local environments. Use hermetic sandboxes—blocking network access and API calls to prevent data exfiltration, with full environment cleanup across runs.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Guard</span>
          <strong>Control side effects.</strong>
          <p>Give the runtime clear working directories, resource limits, and truncated outputs. Treat network access, secrets, and destructive commands as explicit permission boundaries.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>ADK Runtimes</span>
          <strong>Leverage managed executors.</strong>
          <p>Instead of building custom sandboxes, use ADK's integrations like the Vertex Code Interpreter Extension for data analysis, or the Vertex Gemini Enterprise code execution API for server-side safety.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-head compact-head" style="margin-top: 40px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">The protocol</p>
      <h2>How a code-execution turn actually flows.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="mini-diagram react-diagram" aria-label="Code execution flow diagram" style="margin-bottom: 32px;">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 160" role="img">
        <defs>
          <marker id="react-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
            <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <g class="react-links">
          <path d="M 124 56 H 180"></path>
          <path d="M 300 56 H 356"></path>
          <path d="M 476 56 H 532"></path>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg">
          <rect x="24" y="34" width="100" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="74" y="60">Declare</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg is-planner">
          <rect x="180" y="34" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="240" y="60">Emit code</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg">
          <rect x="356" y="34" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="416" y="60">Sandbox</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg is-done">
          <rect x="532" y="34" width="100" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="582" y="60">Observe</text>
        </g>
        <text class="loop-caption" x="320" y="116">model emits code; runtime executes securely</text>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <ul class="timeline-list">
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">01</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Declare the runtime</strong>
          <p>The executor is configured on the agent — a sandbox resource, a working directory, resource limits, and what the environment is allowed to reach (network, filesystem, credentials). Unlike a function tool, the agent's action surface is "anything this runtime can run," so the bounds <em>are</em> the contract.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">02</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Dispatch the block</strong>
          <p>The model emits a code block. The runtime executes it inside the sandbox with the declared limits applied. The model never touches the host process; it produces a string of code, and the executor decides whether and how to run it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">03</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Observe</strong>
          <p>Stdout, stderr, return values, and produced artifacts come back as a structured observation. Good runtimes truncate output so the next turn's context stays bounded — code that prints a million rows shouldn't blow the context window.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">04</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Recover or continue</strong>
          <p>Tracebacks are useful feedback, not failures: the model reads them and tries again. This is the loop's superpower — concrete error messages produce sharper retries than schema-validation errors ever do.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mechanism-note">
      Code execution is the tool form behind CodeAct (chapter 06): when the
      executor is trusted, the model can compose actions inside one block
      instead of one tool call per step. The runtime here is the same; what
      changes upstream is whether the agent treats code as <em>one tool among
      many</em> or as <em>the primary action shape</em>.
    </p>

    <figure class="code-card code-card--standalone">
      <figcaption>
        <span class="lang-pill">python</span>
        <span class="code-filename">google_adk_code_execution.py</span>
        <a href="https://adk.dev/integrations/code-exec-agent-runtime/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google ADK docs</a>
      </figcaption>
      <Code code={codeExecutionCode} lang="python" theme="github-light" />
    </figure>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
